 "notes": [
  "Step 1 was missing one jumper wire. I added wire6 to carry 5V from the top + rail to the button's bottom side. The Uno has one 5V pin, so wire1 feeds the rail and wire6 takes it on to the button.",
  "Circuit follows the schematic. Each sensor sits between 5V and its analog pin (A0 or A1). A 10k resistor from that node to GND forms the voltage divider. The pushbutton connects 5V to digital pin 2, with a 10k pull-down to GND on the pin 2 side.",
  "Breadboard rails: the top + rail (tp) carries 5V and the top - rail (tn) carries GND. Sensor legs and the resistors' ground legs go straight into rail holes.",
  "The button straddles the centre gap in columns 14 and 16. Its side 1 (1.l, 1.r) is on the top half with wire5 (pin 2) and r3 (pull-down) sharing strip 14t. Its side 2 (2.l, 2.r) is on the bottom half and takes 5V from wire6.",
  "Sensors are drawn as two-terminal variable resistors, so I used photoresistors. A potentiometer or FSR could replace them, since the page lists these as alternatives.",
  "The USB cable to the computer and the Processing/Max software are not circuit parts, so they are not in the graph."
 ],
